= Experimental setup <ch:setup>

The experimental setup I worked on during by thesis was built by several generations of PhD students.
While the first iteration of the experimental setup was used to study interactions and collective modes in two-dimensional Fermi gases @feld_low_2011 @frohlich_strongly_2011 @vogt_collective_2013, the setup has since been upgraded by a three-dimensional optical lattice @cocchi_analogue_2016 @miller_ultracold_2016 @drewes_thermodynamics_2020, and a vertical superlattice @chan_quantum_2019 @gall_quantum_2020 @wurz_quantum_2021.
The addition of the optical lattices enabled measurements of the two-dimensional Fermi-Hubbard model @cocchi_equation_2016 @drewes_antiferromagnetic_2017 @wurz_coherent_2018, and the bilayer Fermi-Hubbard model @gall_competing_2021.
During the early stages of the experimental setup, there was already an in-plane superlattice @pertot_relaxation_2014 which was recently set up again @klemmer_ultracold_2020.

In this chapter, I will briefly summarize the experimental setup as of the start of my thesis.
Furthermore, I will introduce the experimental sequence we use to cool the atoms from room temperature to a quantum degenerate Fermi gas, which is then loaded into the optical lattices.
The only major changes to the experimental setup are related to the in-plane superlattice, which I will discuss in detail in @ch:super.
The specific modifications of the experimental sequence are introduced in the respective chapters @ch:mod[] and @ch:phase[].

